JVMTI (Java Virtual Machine Toolkit Interface) is a Java API that allows developers to instrument and debug Java applications. JVMTI provides a way to perform class transformations, which are used to modify the behavior of a Java class at runtime.

Here are some code examples of JVMTI class transformations in Android:

**1. Adding a method to a class**

This example shows how to add a new method to a Java class using JVMTI:
```java
package com.example.jvmti;

import java.lang.instrument;
import java.lang.instrument.ClassTransformer;
import java.lang.instrument.Instrumentation;

public class AddMethodTransformer implements ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public byte[] transform(ClassLoader loader, String className, Class<?> c, final boolean isRetransformation) {
        if (!className.equals("com/example/HelloWorld")) {
            return null;
        }

        // Add a new method to the HelloWorld class
        byte[] classBytes = loader.loadClass(className).getResourceAsStream(className.replace('.', '/') + ".class").readAllBytes();
        ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(classBytes);
        ZipInputStream zis = new ZipInputStream(bais);
        ZipEntry zipEntry = zis.getNextEntry();
        while (zipEntry != null) {
            if (zipEntry.getName().equals("HelloWorld.class")) {
                byte[] classData = new byte[(int) zipEntry.getSize()];
                zis.read(classData);
                String newMethod = "public int foo() {\n" +
                        "    return 42;\n" +
                        "}\n";
                String[] methods = classData.toString().split("AInterface {}0\n");
                StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();
                for (String method : methods) {
                    method = method.trim();
                    if (method.startsWith("public int ")) {
                        // Add the new method
                        builder.append(newMethod);
                    } else {
                        builder.append(method);
                    }
                }
                classData = builder.toString().getBytes();
                zis.close();
                return classData;
            }
            zipEntry = zis.getNextEntry();
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```
This transformer adds a new method `foo()` to the `com.example.HelloWorld` class.

**2. Changing the implementation of a method**

This example shows how to change the implementation of a method using JVMTI:
```java
